in the heart of the mississippi delta a  high school football team and its  unlikely success is captivating the  state with barely enough players to take  the field greenville christian academy  has knocked out football powerhouses and  is ranked number one in mississippi jan  crawford traveled to greenville and  discovered that a school born in an era  of hate and division is now all about  unity  here in the mississippi delta  these kids know what it's like to be  underestimated  we've always been the underdogs no one  really looked towards us  their football field is surrounded by a  soybean farm  the weight room looks like an unfinished  garage because you got big facilities  great feel great they don't matter  what they have is each other  we trust each other i trust them they  trust me i got their back they got my  back  that trust has helped these young men at  this tiny private school pull off the  impossible  greenville christian academy has run the  gauntlet against mississippi's top teams  private and public and come out number  one  they're balling they're ballroom  knocking off the top academy teams in  the state  and that's what's even more incredible  in the deep south the academy teams are  the private schools created for white  kids only  after the supreme court ordered public  schools to desegregate greenville  academy which opened in 1969 was one of  them white only  today while other academies now admit  black students greenville academy is 70  percent black it's a lot of white people  and black people coming together and we  all just know each other so we just feel  like we're at home  [Applause]  family atmosphere and christian values  are what the school strives for and the  belief that you can do great things  smith will keep it that's why star  quarterback deandre smith and others  decided last year to come to greenville  christian we bonded like a family we do  everything together smith's old school  canceled its season last year because of  covet he could have gone to fancier  private schools with better facilities  but he was drawn to the atmosphere of  greenville christian from day one i knew  it was going to be a perfect fit for me  because when i first came in  it felt like we knew each other for all  our lives dj smith hands to palmer there  was no resentment from kids already here  like senior running back marlon palmer  we all knew each other but like  come as a team it just feels like we  just reunited again and lineman wendell  poindexter who like palmer started  greenville christian in sixth grade they  helped convince smith and others to come  accepted everyone we wanted everyone to  be a part of us  just making family  the small coaching staff with this  shoestring budget all are graduates of  the school  john reed mclendon is the head coach  let's go fellas justin levy was just a  couple years behind him  jordan mcclendon is the head coach's  younger brother it's just going to show  you that you know a lot of times and you  know the nice things don't really matter  who's your strength and conditioning  coach i am okay who's your trainer  both of us who does scheduling for you  guys  i mean like  you're beating teams that have you know  a staff of like 10 people doing that and  100 kids on the field how many kids do  you guys have 35 35 36 36  these coaches say what's happening here  goes beyond football they planted the  seeds more than six years ago when they  came back and started coaching together  this school uh seems to  you know reel you in i guess just the  fact that people invested in your life  and you want to come back and invest in  other people's lives it's family it's  home just where my heart is my heart is  greenville it's the delta and it was an  opportunity for me to feel like i was  making an impact there's so many  different ways to be successful in  football football is just like an avenue  to reach other people to me honestly  because that's what i'm using it for  pretty stretch  levy was among the first black students  to enroll at greenville christian i  think we had the first group  and my friends would have decided to  come over i think it was like three or  four of us a star wide receiver in  college levy turned down an opportunity  to play in the canadian football league  choosing instead to come home this is  definitely a once in a lifetime type  thing and are you talking about the  success that you've had on the football  field the success and like how it all  happened you know  just seeing some of the kids live being  transformed i just think every kid is  here supposed to be here and god has  blessed us tremendously it's really a  blessing because  a lot of people don't have this  opportunity we have and like  we  they tell our coach tell us every day to  cherish it  [Applause]  a blessing that allows these kids to  dream of great things  i'm a kid from a small town with big  dreams like you couldn't even imagine  what i'd be thinking about so  it's crazy  [Applause]  [Music]  now deandre and four other greenville  christian players have offers to play  division one college football from  alcorn state to michigan state and  others will go on to smaller colleges  all  great things nate  jan awesome stuff sports being a  catalyst for change in a place that  definitely needs it appreciate you yeah  awesome story five d1 offers yeah that's  serious and to see the coaches come back  um after they've you know graduated but  how important it was for them to give  back to those students today and it's  one thing to have a small team but they  also have a  small  coaching staff yeah yeah three  individuals on that coaching staff and  they're the best in the state you know  i'm sure people are going to see this  hear about how they're winning and give  them some money and they're going to  have a nicer weight room but i kind of  want them to keep that old garage look  to it keep the gritty feel right yeah  it's not bad it helps